WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Random forest.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.437
One tree memorizes.

3
00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:07.800
A whole forest of differently wrong trees cancels its own mistakes.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.667
A single tree memorizes its training data.

5
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.067
A forest trains many trees, and makes each one overfit in a different way.

6
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:19.800
Then it averages their votes, and the errors largely cancel.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Here is the recipe, called bagging.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.833
Start with the training table.

9
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:28.833
Each tree gets a bootstrap sample, rows drawn with replacement.

10
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:33.867
Each tree also considers only a random subset of features at every split.

11
00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:38.233
So the trees come out diverse, and their votes are averaged into one answer.

12
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:43.033
That is why it is the reliable default for tabular data.

13
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:45.233
It is strong with almost no tuning.

14
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:48.967
The trees are independent, so training spreads across cores.

15
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.767
And you get feature importances and out of bag error for free.

16
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:55.833
Let's compare them in scikit-learn.

17
00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:58.133
First, a single unlimited tree.

18
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:00.300
Then a forest of two hundred trees.

19
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.135
Same data, same split.

20
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:05.067
The forest usually wins by a clear margin.

21
00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.100
Here is the exoplanet classifier's first version.

22
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.833
It ships on a two hundred tree forest, with no tuning beyond the number of trees.

23
00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:18.933
It reaches zero point nine AUC on four features, and trains in seconds.

24
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:23.867
The feature importances double as the explanation slide for the science team.

25
00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:25.827
Let's recap.

26
00:01:25.827 --> 00:01:28.233
Train many trees on bootstrap samples.

27
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:30.933
Random feature subsets add diversity.

28
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:33.467
Averaging the votes reduces variance.

29
00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:37.325
It is the reliable default for tabular data.

30
00:01:37.325 --> 00:01:38.700
Next up: XGBoost.
